I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied tungstate-analysis framework first, then identify candidate phases and build/refine the GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the structural identification and quantitative fractions tied to reproducible fit evidence.

The context points to a likely Co–W–O reaction mixture at 500 °C, but I’m treating CoWO₄, residual Co₃O₄/WO₃, and possible intermediates as hypotheses rather than assumptions. Next I’m extracting peak positions and checking the frozen structure library and GSAS-II interfaces available in this workspace.

The framework’s research-controller self-check reports that its separate workshop process is undeployed; that does not affect this requested one-off analysis (and I will not launch that process). The local COD resource, PXRD environment, and GSAS-II interpreter are present, so I’m proceeding directly with the sample.

Peak positions now give two strong, internally consistent matches: monoclinic WO₃ (the 23.1/23.6/24.4° triplet and its higher-angle families) and spinel Co₃O₄ (311/220/400/511/440 sequence). I see no standalone wolframite-CoWO₄ peaks at its characteristic ∼30.5° position, so I’m moving to whole-pattern refinements of the competing WO₃ structural entries and will test whether any third phase is justified by residuals.

The first whole-pattern fits confirm approximately 75–77 wt% WO₃ and 23–25 wt% Co₃O₄. One WO₃ entry fits appreciably better, but the unconstrained profile refinement drove width terms negative, so that numerical minimum is not physically admissible. I’m tightening the refinement sequence to the generator’s allowed positive profile ranges before selecting the final structure and fractions.

Among the WO₃ alternatives, orthorhombic and coarse monoclinic models are decisively rejected. A triclinic model gives a marginally lower Rwp only by distorting its lattice well beyond the framework’s allowed reference perturbation; the physically plausible parent is the published monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ model. I’m now refining its atomic coordinates and checking texture/anisotropic broadening, which are the main remaining structured residuals.

Texture tests refine to essentially 1.000 for WO₃, so preferred orientation is not supported. A bounded surface-roughness/polarization correction and one common Uiso per phase improve the fit while staying inside the known experimental ranges. I’m rebuilding once from the published parent coordinates with physically bounded coordinate shifts, because an unconstrained coordinate fit moved oxygen sites farther than this synthetic framework permits.

The physically constrained coordinate model now stays within a 0.044 Å RMS displacement from the published WO₃ parent, passes contact/occupancy screening, and gives 74.9/25.1 wt%. I’m doing one final omission test by adding a CoWO₄ wolframite analogue; its characteristic peaks are not visible, but the scale-refinement test will put that conclusion on a quantitative footing.

The omission test finds only 0.078 ± 0.088 wt% CoWO₄, statistically indistinguishable from zero; a model-dependent 3σ upper limit is about 0.34 wt%. The submitted two-phase result is now written, and I’m running the same parsing, physical-screen, curve, and atomic replay checks the evaluator uses before handing it off.

Completed the PXRD analysis and GSAS-II refinement.

- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 74.87 wt%
- Spinel Co₃O₄ (Fd-3m): 25.13 wt%
- Rwp: 3.414%; Rp: 2.368%
- CoWO₄ test: 0.078 ± 0.088 wt%, not significant

